What happened
On Monday, November 2, 2026, U.S. stocks ended mixed, and technology companies took most of the damage. The Nasdaq Composite, an index packed with tech firms, lost about 1.07% and closed at 25,164.32 points. The S&P 500 slipped about 0.5%, while the Dow Jones Industrial Average actually gained about 0.4%.

Index	Monday, Nov 2	What it tracks
Nasdaq Composite	about -1.07% (closed at 25,164.32)	Many tech and growth companies
S&P 500	about -0.5%	500 of the largest U.S. companies
Dow Jones	about +0.4%	30 large, well-known companies
The Nasdaq was already weak: it fell roughly 5.3% during October. According to MarketWatch, the Nasdaq-100 (a related index of the 100 biggest Nasdaq companies) is now about 10.4% below its record high from October 6. That puts it in what traders call correction territory.

Why is tech falling?
Analysts point to a few connected reasons:

Big spending on artificial intelligence (AI). Tech companies are spending billions on data centers, advanced chips and cloud computing. Investors are asking whether that spending will turn into enough profit to be worth it.
Disappointing news from some companies. Intel shared a weaker-than-expected outlook, which made people worry about the chip industry as a whole.
Higher expectations. Even good results can push a stock down if investors were hoping for something even better. Apple, for example, reported about $114.5 billion in quarterly revenue and $30.7 billion in net income, yet its shares still came under pressure.
What is a market correction?
A correction happens when an index falls at least 10% from a recent peak. It sounds scary, but it does not automatically mean a recession or a long "bear market." It usually means investors are reconsidering how much stocks should be worth. Corrections have happened many times in market history, and the Nasdaq-100 has now gone through its third one this year.

Key words
Index: a group of stocks used to measure how a part of the market is doing.
Nasdaq: a U.S. stock exchange known for technology companies.
Valuation: how much investors think a company is worth compared with what it earns.
Correction: a drop of 10% or more from a recent high.
Earnings report: a company's public update on its sales and profit.
Futures: contracts that can hint at how the market might open before trading starts.
What to watch next
According to Investopedia, these events could affect the market in the coming days:

U.S. midterm elections: a change in who controls Congress could affect economic and business policies.
AMD's earnings report: due after the market closes on Tuesday, it will show whether demand for AI-related technology is holding up.
Jobs data: new employment reports can shape expectations about economic growth and Federal Reserve decisions.
More tech earnings: investors want to see whether big companies can back up their high valuations with real results.
Early Tuesday morning, Nasdaq-100 futures were up about 0.3%, which hinted at a small recovery at the open. Remember that futures are only an early signal, not a guarantee.

What we can learn
Stock prices move on expectations, not only on today's results. Good numbers can still lead to a drop if people expected more.
The market does not move as one block. On the same day, tech fell while the Dow rose.
A 10% drop is a normal, if uncomfortable, part of investing, and it is not by itself proof that the economy is collapsing.
This is also why diversification and a long-term view are popular topics in investing education. We are explaining the ideas here, not telling anyone what to buy or sell.
